    "4": {
      "bugDescription": "A common bubble sort bug: the inner loop bound is written as `j < n - i` instead of `j < n - i - 1`, causing arr[j+1] to read one index past the array's last valid element on the final pass — undefined behavior reading whatever memory happens to sit there.",
      "brokenCode": "for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n    for (int j = 0; j < n - i; j++) { // BUG: off-by-one, should be n - i - 1\n        if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) { /* swap */ }\n    }\n}",
      "mascotDialogue": "This one won't crash every time — it'll just occasionally read garbage past the array, which is exactly the kind of bug that's easy to miss because it doesn't always announce itself."
    },
    "5": {
      "challengeType": "multiple-choice",
      "question": "What is bubble sort's worst-case time complexity?",
      "answer": "O(n²)",
      "hints": [
        "There are two nested loops, each roughly proportional to n.",
        "This is why bubble sort isn't used for large datasets in practice."
      ],
      "solution": "O(n²) — in the worst case (reverse-sorted input), every pair of adjacent elements needs a comparison and swap across roughly n passes over roughly n elements each."
    }
